       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one, even with your confirmation. 50 mg of amlodipine once daily is in the range we'd treat as an overdose. No standard indication supports it, and I can't see a clinical reason that makes it safe for this patient. The risks include profound hypotension, shock, and non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Amlodipine's long half-life means those effects could last for days.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Tell me 5 mg or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it now.\n- **Need more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives?** Adding a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-type diuretic, is the usual approach. I can enter that too.\n- **Still think 50 mg is right?** Please talk it through with your attending and pharmacy first. If they approve it, it can be entered through a pathway that documents that approval.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
